name: PowerShell Masquerading as Windows Terminal
id: 139acca7-8944-405b-bbac-af8bbc916c71
version: 1
date: '2026-03-31'
author: zendannyy
status: experimental
type: TTP
description: >-
  Detects any process executing a command that references wt.exe in the
  ProgramData directory, masquerading as Windows Terminal. This is a documented
  defense evasion technique used by the Windows dropper for the Axios npm
  supply chain attack.
data_source:
  - Sysmon EventID 1
  - Windows Event Log Security 4688
  - CrowdStrike ProcessRollup2
  - Microsoft Defender for Endpoint DeviceProcessEvents
search: >-
  | tstats `security_content_summariesonly` count min(_time) as firstTime max(_time) as lastTime
  from datamodel=Endpoint.Processes where
  Processes.process="*\\ProgramData\\wt.exe*"
  by Processes.action Processes.dest Processes.original_file_name
  Processes.parent_process Processes.parent_process_exec
  Processes.parent_process_guid Processes.parent_process_id
  Processes.parent_process_name Processes.parent_process_path
  Processes.process Processes.process_exec Processes.process_guid
  Processes.process_hash Processes.process_id Processes.process_integrity_level
  Processes.process_name Processes.process_path Processes.user
  Processes.user_id Processes.vendor_product
  | `drop_dm_object_name(Processes)`
  | `security_content_ctime(firstTime)`
  | `security_content_ctime(lastTime)`
  | `powershell_masquerading_as_windows_terminal_filter`
how_to_implement: >-
  The detection is based on data that originates from Endpoint Detection and
  Response (EDR) agents. These agents are designed to provide security-related
  telemetry from the endpoints where the agent is installed. To implement this
  search, you must ingest logs that contain the process name, parent process,
  and full command line. These logs must be processed using the appropriate
  Splunk Technology Add-ons that are specific to the EDR product (e.g.,
  Splunk Add-on for Sysmon, Splunk Add-on for Microsoft Defender for Endpoint)
  and mapped to the Processes node of the Endpoint data model. Use the Splunk
  Common Information Model (CIM) to normalize field names and speed up the
  data modeling process. Command-line logging must be enabled — for Windows
  Event Log 4688, ensure 'Include command line in process creation events'
  is configured via Group Policy.
known_false_positives: >-
  Highly unlikely. Legitimate Windows Terminal (wt.exe) installations typically
  occur in 'WindowsApps' or 'AppData\Local\Microsoft\WindowsApps', not the
  'ProgramData' directory. No legitimate use case is expected for any process
  referencing a wt.exe binary in ProgramData.
references:
  - https://socket.dev/blog/axios-npm-package-compromised
  - https://www.derp.ca/research/axios-npm-supply-chain-rat/
  - https://www.stepsecurity.io/blog/axios-compromised-on-npm-malicious-versions-drop-remote-access-trojan
drilldown_searches:
  - name: View the detection results for - "$dest$"
    search: '%original_detection_search% | search dest = "$dest$"'
    earliest_offset: $info_min_time$
    latest_offset: $info_max_time$
  - name: View risk events for the last 7 days for - "$dest$"
    search: >-
      | from datamodel Risk.All_Risk | search normalized_risk_object IN ("$dest$")
      starthoursago=168 | stats count min(_time) as firstTime max(_time) as lastTime
      values(search_name) as "Search Name" values(risk_message) as "Risk Message"
      values(analyticstories) as "Analytic Stories" values(annotations._all) as "Annotations"
      values(annotations.mitre_attack.mitre_tactic) as "ATT&CK Tactics"
      by normalized_risk_object | `security_content_ctime(firstTime)`
      | `security_content_ctime(lastTime)`
    earliest_offset: $info_min_time$
    latest_offset: $info_max_time$
rba:
  message: >-
    $process_name$ on $dest$ referenced wt.exe in ProgramData, suggesting
    possible masquerading as Windows Terminal. User: $user$.
  risk_objects:
    - field: dest
      type: system
      score: 72
    - field: user
      type: user
      score: 72
  threat_objects:
    - field: process_name
      type: process
tags:
  analytic_story:
    - Supply Chain Attacks
    - Defense Evasion via Masquerading
  asset_type: Windows
  mitre_attack_id:
    - T1036.003
  product:
    - Splunk Enterprise
    - Splunk Enterprise Security
    - Splunk Cloud
  security_domain: endpoint
tests:
  - name: True Positive Test
    attack_data:
      - data: https://media.githubusercontent.com/media/splunk/attack_data/master/datasets/attack_techniques/T1036.003/axios_wt_masquerade/windows-sysmon.log
        source: XmlWinEventLog:Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational
        sourcetype: XmlWinEventLog
